<h2>Blueprint</h2>
<Blueprint />
<p class="hint">
    Drag to orbit, scroll to zoom. Click an assembly to isolate it. <strong>Drawer</strong> runs the
    electronics tray out of the fourth face; <strong>Explode</strong> separates the stack along the
    service axis. The three risers and both bay plates move together, because they are one welded
    assembly.
</p>

<h2 id="figures">Key figures</h2>
<div class="build-specs">
    <div><dt>Section</dt><dd>8 × 8 <small>in internal</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Wall</dt><dd>¼ <small>in cast acrylic</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Nest</dt><dd>12 <small>in tall</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Bay</dt><dd>4½ <small>in tall</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Outworld</dt><dd>8 <small>in tall</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Overall</dt><dd>26 <small>in / 660 mm</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Base</dt><dd>16 × 16 <small>in, ballasted</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Tip angle</dt><dd>≈45 <small>deg</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Risers</dt><dd>3 × ¾ <small>in OD, ½ in bore</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Bay plates</dt><dd>10 × 10 <small>in, ¾ in flange</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Drawer</dt><dd>3¾ × 2¾ <small>in opening</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Supported load</dt><dd>≈5 <small>lb, designed to 25</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Reservoir</dt><dd>131 <small>mL</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Heater</dt><dd>20 <small>W at 12 V DC</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Vent mesh</dt><dd>&lt;0.5 <small>mm aperture</small></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Refill interval</dt><dd>3–4 <small>weeks</small></dd></div>
</div>
<p class="hint">
    Tip angle is <code>atan(half-base / centre-of-mass height)</code>, and it is dominated by where
    the ballast sits rather than by how tall the column is — which is why the base is both wider
    than it looks like it needs to be and heavy at the very bottom.
</p>

<h2 id="load">How the load comes down</h2>
<p class="lede">
    The outworld, its substrate and the lid — about five pounds — sit above a bay that has to be
    open on one side. Everything about the middle of this column follows from resolving those two
    facts.
</p>

<div class="build-note">
    <div class="build-eyebrow">The path, top to bottom</div>
    <p>
        Lid and outworld walls clamp onto the <strong>outworld floor plate</strong>. That plate is
        a 10 in square that overhangs the column by ¾ in on every side. The overhang is the
        landing for the riser collars. Load leaves the plate through <strong>three ¾ in OD acrylic
        risers</strong>, on the midlines of three faces, and through a <strong>portal frame</strong>
        of two stiles and a header on the fourth. It arrives at the <strong>nest ceiling plate</strong>,
        an identical 10 in square, and from there into the nest walls and the base.
    </p>
</div>

<p>
    Each riser passes through both plates and is solvent-welded with a collar against all four
    faces. That is a bearing joint, not a seal that happens to hold: a 2 in collar against a ¾ in
    tube is 2.7 in² of annulus, and there are two per plate — so 5.4 in² of weld per plate per riser,
    against a service load under two pounds each. The tubes are not sized by
    strength — they are sized by the ½ in bore an ant needs and by the collar land the weld wants.
    What matters is that the load has one declared path and it is a short, straight, welded one.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>The bay enclosure carries nothing.</strong> It is a 5¼ in box standing in the middle of
    the plates, a waist inside the column line, and its only jobs are to keep light and dust off the
    electronics. That is precisely why one of its four faces can be a hole. Three faces are closed
    panels with a riser standing outside each; the fourth is the drawer.
</p>

<p>
    Three supports on a four-sided plate put the load resultant off-axis, which is what the portal
    frame is for. Its two stiles are welded between the plates at the edges of the drawer opening
    and take the fourth quarter of the load, so the plate is supported at four points and the drawer
    slides through a frame that is already carrying its share. Those stiles are what fixes the
    opening width: the enclosure is 4¾ in inside, the stiles take ½ in each side, and what is left —
    3¾ in — is the opening the 3½ in tray passes through. The drawer itself is a tray on rails
    behind a gasketed face plate on two captive thumbscrews. Pull it and nothing structural moves,
    nothing in the ant path is touched, and no weld is disturbed.
</p>

<div class="build-note">
    <div class="build-eyebrow">What rides the drawer, and what does not</div>
    <p>
        <strong>On the drawer:</strong> the ESP32 and the perfboard carrying all five MOSFET stages,
        terminal blocks and passives — everything with firmware or a failure mode worth swapping.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Bonded in the bay:</strong> the three LED rings in their recesses, the nest exhaust
        fan over its port, and the bulkhead connector strip. These are dumb and they are aligned to
        holes in the plates; re-aligning them is a worse job than replacing one in place.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>On the base, outside the column:</strong> both peristaltic pumps and the supply jar.
        Plumbing does not belong in a four-inch bay, and on the base it is reachable without opening
        anything.
    </p>
</div>

<p>
    The outworld is the mirror image of the same rule. Its floor is welded to the risers and cannot
    move, so the four walls and the lid lift off that floor as one unit, seating on a continuous
    gasket in the plate's rebate under toggle clamps. Cleaning is: plug the three risers with their
    manual caps, unclamp, lift, wash, reseat. The nest below is never opened at all once the colony
    is in — it is ventilated through a mesh-screened port in its ceiling and a passive mesh intake
    low on its wall, giving bottom-to-top cross-flow along the thermal gradient with nothing
    mechanical inside it.
</p>

<h2 id="parts">Parts</h2>
<p class="lede">
    Full sourcing, prices and a costed total in <code>docs/bom.md</code>. Links and prices were last
    checked on <strong>3 September 2026</strong>; the planning total is roughly
    <strong>$560–640</strong> excluding tools, dominated by the acrylic sheet and the pair of
    peristaltic pumps. Prices marked ✔ below were read off the vendor's own page — everything else
    is an estimate, not a quote.
</p>

<div class="build-note danger">
    <div class="build-eyebrow">Settle the nest core before cutting any acrylic</div>
    <p>
        <strong>One standard block is exactly enough.</strong> The commodity 4 in AAC block is
        24 × 8 × 4 in; halved and trimmed to 10.5 × 7.75 in it gives both slabs, and two of them back
        to back fill the 8 in section precisely. One block, two cuts, almost no waste.
    </p>
    <p>
        Buying one is the hard part. Raw aerated concrete is a European commodity and is hard to get
        in small quantities in the US — the domestic makers sell by the pallet, and what the
        ant-keeping market stocks instead are finished Ytong nests around 108 × 63 × 28 mm, an order
        of magnitude too small. eBay is where keepers actually get it; Aercon is worth an email
        first. The fallback is a poured medium — hydrostone, or plaster and sand — which wicks, is
        rigid and pours to any dimension, but is heavier, cannot be re-carved, and this design has
        not been checked against it.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Not firebrick.</strong> K-23 insulating brick carves beautifully and wicks, and it is
        the substitute the US forums reach for — but it throws an extremely fine dust, and colonies
        held in it for extended periods are reported to go sluggish and die without another
        explanation. This nest is never opened again once the colony is in, which is exactly the
        exposure it fails at.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Every internal dimension in the build is set by the core</strong>, so this is the
        first purchase, not the last.
    </p>
</div>

