<h2 id="sequence">Build sequence</h2>
<p class="lede">
    @BuildSequence.StepCount steps, ordered so that nothing has to be undone. Progress is stored on
    the server, not in this browser, so a step ticked on a phone at the bench is ticked everywhere.
</p>

<BuildChecklist />

<h2 id="bringup">Bring-up order</h2>
<p class="lede">
    One subsystem at a time on the breadboard, each proved before the next is added. Every one of
    these has a failure mode that looks like a different subsystem's fault if you wire it all at once.
</p>

<div class="scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Subsystem</th><th>Prove it by</th><th>If it fails</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Power</td><td>12 V at the rail, 5 V out of the buck, 3V3 on the ESP32</td><td>Check the buck's output before anything else is connected to it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1-Wire</td><td>Boot log prints three ROM IDs</td><td>Confirm the 4.7 kΩ pull-up and that RX <em>and</em> TX both reach DQ</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Probe identity</td><td>Warm one probe in a closed hand; watch which ID moves</td><td>Record each ID in <code>DeviceConfig</code>. Never trust bus order.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>I2C</td><td>Both SHT31s respond, at 0x44 and 0x45</td><td>Only one answering means the ADDR pin is unset on one board</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Soil probe</td><td>Note raw counts in air, then in saturated Ytong</td><td>Those two numbers are the calibration. Higher counts mean <em>drier</em>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Float switch</td><td>Continuity closes as the float rises</td><td>Verify LOW means full before the pump is ever enabled</td></tr>
            <tr><td>MOSFETs</td><td>Each output switches a dummy load; all off at boot</td><td>A missing 10 kΩ pulldown shows up as an output on during reset</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Beams</td><td>Wave a card through each; counts increment independently</td><td>A high debounced-count ratio means dirty or misaligned optics</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rings</td><td>Each ring lights its own channel colour</td><td>All three lit the same means the chain order does not match riser order</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Nest exhaust</td><td>Airflow at the ceiling port with the intake unblocked</td><td>No flow usually means the intake mesh is sealed against the sleeve</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Drawer</td><td>Pull to the detent and push home with the column powered</td><td>Anything that tugs is a service loop cut too short — fix it before the colony arrives</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<div class="build-note danger">
    <div class="build-eyebrow">Before the colony arrives</div>
    <p>
        Run the whole column empty for <strong>two weeks</strong>. Confirm the nest holds 79–83 °F
        overnight, that a refill cycle completes and the reservoir reads full, that the substrate
        stabilises rather than trending, and that no fault has latched. Order the colony only after
        that. The failures that matter in this system are slow ones, and two weeks is the shortest
        window in which a slow failure becomes visible.
    </p>
</div>

<h2 id="experiment">The experiment</h2>
<p>
    Each riser mouth is lit from below through a frosted ring-window in the outworld floor, in one of
    three channels, at a brightness that tracks that riser's own traffic. Traffic changes the light at
    the choice point; the light may change the traffic.
</p>

<div class="build-note">
    <div class="build-eyebrow">What it actually measures</div>
    <p>
        Ants are most sensitive to UV and blue-green and are effectively <strong>blind to deep red</strong>.
        So these are not three flavours of one stimulus — they are three very different ones. The
        prediction is negative feedback on blue, weak feedback on green, and almost none on red, which
        means <strong>traffic should drift toward whichever riser is currently showing red</strong>.
    </p>
</div>

<p class="build-channels">
    <span><i style="background:var(--chan-red)"></i>Red — near-invisible, so near-zero deterrence</span>
    <span><i style="background:var(--chan-green)"></i>Green — visible, moderate deterrence</span>
    <span><i style="background:var(--chan-blue)"></i>Blue — highly visible, strongest deterrence</span>
</p>

<h3>Three conditions, and one control that makes it mean anything</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Off</strong> — dark. Traffic logged with no light at all: the baseline.</li>
    <li><strong>Fixed</strong> — every ring lit equally. Colour present, carrying no information about
        traffic. This is what separates "ants respond to colour" from "ants respond to light changing at all".</li>
    <li><strong>Closed loop</strong> — the treatment.</li>
</ul>

<p>
    The load-bearing control is the <strong>rotation</strong>: the riser-to-channel mapping advances at
    local midnight on a three-day cycle, and every traffic row in the log carries the channel and
    brightness that were in force when it was counted. Without it, "they prefer green" and "they prefer
    the tube nearest the food dish" produce identical data and the experiment means nothing. The
    <a href="traffic">Traffic</a> page shows both groupings side by side for exactly this reason.
</p>

<p>
    A guaranteed dark period runs 22:00–07:00 local regardless of mode or activity, so the colony always
    has the option of darkness.
</p>

